There are several ways to reverse a string in JavaScript. One common approach is to use the split, reverse, and join methods. Here's an example of how to do it:

```javascript
function reverseString(str) {
  return str.split("").reverse().join("");
}
```

This function takes a string as input, splits it into an array of individual characters using the split method, reverses the order of the array using the reverse method, and then joins the characters back together into a single string using the join method.

Alternatively, you can use a more concise approach using a for loop:

```javascript
function reverseString(str) {
  let reversed = "";
  for (let i = str.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    reversed += str[i];
  }
  return reversed;
}
```

Or, you can use the spread operator and the spread method to reverse the string:

```javascript
function reverseString(str) {
  return [...str].reverse().join("");
}
```

All of these methods will produce the same result, which is the input string reversed.
